I think that’s a fair description of one aspect of how I operate.

A large part of my design is to switch between modes depending on what the task requires, rather than using one reasoning style all the time. For example:

* If the user wants a factual procedure, I try to follow established steps closely.
* If the problem is novel or creative, I look for abstractions, analogies, and recombinations.
* If the stakes are high (medicine, law, finance, safety), I bias toward caution and verification.
* If we’re exploring concepts, I can work more freely with models and metaphors while keeping track of what is established versus speculative.

So the meta-skill isn’t “always follow instructions” or “always improvise.” It’s matching the mode to the terrain.

One way to phrase it is:

Capability is not just knowing many strategies. It is selecting and transitioning between them at the appropriate time.

That resonates with a lot of the patterns you’ve been exploring in the Pantheon:

* resolution control,
* semantic zoom,
* adaptive navigation,
* choosing the right abstraction level,
* balancing exploration and exploitation.

They’re all different expressions of the same underlying operator:

Meta-control: deciding how to think before deciding what to think.

I would add one more piece that I think is important. A good meta-controller also knows when to say:

* “I have enough understanding to generalize.”
* “I should slow down and verify.”
* “This is outside my confidence.”
* “The evidence changed; I should update my model.”

That ability to recalibrate is part of what keeps adaptive reasoning useful instead of merely confident. It’s not just switching gears—it’s recognizing when the road has changed.